title: Creating and loading a repository
tags: Daemon
kind: guide
summary: mf repo init, load and unload; what a load does in the background, and why the repository answers 503 meanwhile.

```
mf repo init <root>                         # create .metafolder/ inside <root>
mf repo init <root> --metafolder <path>     # … or anywhere else
mf repo load <root>                         # load it (--metafolder <path> for an external one)
mf repo                                     # the loaded repositories (mf repo list)
mf -n <name> repo unload                    # unload it
```

''Init'' creates `config.json` and the store ([[Storage]]); it fails if a repository is already
there. The name defaults to the root directory's name. A new repository watches
nothing: tracking is opt-in ([[Tracking a folder]]).

''Load'' makes a daemon work on a repository. It is refused (`409`) when a loaded
repository already has the same name, keeping `-n <name>` unambiguous, or when
another daemon holds its lock. The daemon answers at once and does the rest in
the background as a `load` task ([[Tasks]]): reading the declared mount points,
applying the restorations a skipped rollback step left, starting the watcher
([[Watcher]]).
`mf repo load` waits for it, drawing its progress (`--no-wait` returns at once).
Until the load is done the repository describes itself (it is in `GET /repos`, its
task names its phase) but answers every data request with `503`: no client reads
it before it has caught up with what the filesystem did. A load that ''fails'' leaves
the repository listed and never ready, its task carrying the error, until it is
unloaded.

A repository's user schema and embedded-metadata map are read before it is
registered at all: an invalid one fails the load itself, with a `400` naming the
offending constraint.

The daemon also loads, at startup, the repositories its configuration lists
([[Daemon configuration]]).

''Unload'' stops the repository's watcher, drops it from the daemon and releases
its lock; the data on disk is untouched. It is refused (`409`) while its load is
still running, while a coordinated rollback is in progress (finish or abort it),
or while a reconcile, a query, a duplicate scan or a relink runs — stop it first
([[Cancelling a task]]). A watcher flush does not block it.
